<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hamar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hamar is a town in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> on the shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-mjosa">Lake Mjøsa</a>, the administrative centre of Innlandet county. It became the seat of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> in 1152 and grew around its cathedral, whose ruins are now protected under a glass shelter. The medieval town declined after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, and a new town was founded on the site in 1849.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hamar.jpg" alt="Hamar" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bjørn Christian Tørrissen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hamar-Aerial-2022.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Hamar lies in the traditional region of Hedmarken on the shore of Mjøsa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>'s largest lake, in the south-western part of Hamar Municipality; its built-up area reaches into the neighbouring municipalities of Ringsaker and Stange. The name comes from the old Hamar farm, Old Norse Hamarr, a word meaning rocky hill, on whose land the medieval market was first built. Its coat of arms, a black grouse on a pine tree, was granted on 2 June 1896, but an older version was already described in the Hamar Chronicle of 1553.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Between 500 and 1000 the nearby Åker farm was one of the main centres of power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>. Presumably after 1030, the centre moved to the peninsula now called Domkirkeodden, perhaps on the initiative of Harald Hardråde, who held property there. The town is said to have been at its height in the early 14th century, dominated by the cathedral, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s manor and a fortress, and known for its apple orchards as well as its merchants, craftsmen and fishermen.</p><p>By 1587 merchants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Christiania</a> had drawn all of Hamar's market trade to their own town, and Hamar lost its status as a kjøpstad; by the 17th century it had ceased to exist as a town and its site was farmed as Storhamar. Frederick III gave the estate to Hannibal Sehested in 1649. Plans for a new trading town on Mjøsa date from 1755, and the modern town was laid out on a grid by the army engineer Røyem. Johannes Bay arrived in October 1849 to organise its first elections, and Christian Borchgrevink became its first mayor. In 1860 the lake flooded the front streets, the worst flood recorded since 1789.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Hedmark museum on Domkirkeodden shows the remains of the medieval cathedral under a protective glass housing, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s fortress and old farmhouses. Hamar is a railway junction between two lines from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trondheim">Trondheim</a> and home of the Norwegian Railway Museum, and it hosted figure skating, short track and speed skating at the 1994 Winter Olympics.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hamar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hedmark became part of the Kingdom of Norway, and <b>Hamar</b>, on Lake Mjøsa, became the seat of a bishop in 1152.</blockquote><a href="/state/hedmark">Hedmark</a></li></ul></section>
